course: religion-ethics
lesson: 3
title: Noah and the Tower of Babel
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Genesis tells of Noah, a good man in a violent world, whom God told to build a huge wooden boat, the ark, for his family and pairs of every kind of animal.
explain: Rain fell for forty days and the waters covered the earth; when the rain stopped, Noah sent out a dove, which came back with an olive leaf to show the land was drying.
explain: God then set a rainbow in the clouds as a sign of his covenant, a solemn promise, never again to destroy the earth by a flood.
explain: Later, proud people tried to build a tower to reach heaven and make a name for themselves; God confused their language and scattered them, and the story explains why people speak many languages.
explain: Christians disagree about whether the flood covered the whole world, but they agree on its lesson: God takes wrongdoing seriously, rescues those who trust him and keeps his promises.
explain: For a child today: doing right can mean being different from everyone around you, a promise is a serious thing, and showing off to be the greatest tends to split people apart.
fact: Noah was a good man in a violent world whom God told to build a great boat, the ark.
fact: The ark carried Noah's family and pairs of every kind of animal through the flood.
fact: In the story, rain fell for forty days and the waters covered the earth; everything outside the ark was lost.
fact: The first time Noah sent out a dove it came back with nothing, as there was nowhere dry to rest; the next time it came back with an olive leaf, showing the land was drying.
fact: The dove and the olive branch became symbols of peace and new beginnings.
fact: God set a rainbow in the clouds as a sign of his promise never again to destroy the earth by flood.
fact: A covenant is a solemn promise or agreement; in the Bible God made covenants with Noah, Abraham, Moses and David.
fact: Some Christians read the flood as a worldwide event, others as a local flood or a teaching story (disputed).
fact: Noah is honoured in Islam too, where he is called Nuh.
fact: Stories of great floods appear in many ancient cultures, for example in the Epic of Gilgamesh from Mesopotamia.
fact: At Babel people tried to build a tower to reach heaven and make a name for themselves.
fact: God confused their language and scattered them over the earth.
fact: The Babel story explains why people speak different languages.
fact: Christians see Pentecost, when Jesus' followers were understood in many languages, as the reversal of Babel.
exercise: Who was Noah? | A good man in a violent world whom God told to build an ark | A king who built a tower to reach heaven | A fisherman who lived by a lake in Galilee and followed Jesus | The first man God made in the garden
exercise: What does Noah's story teach about doing right? | It can mean being different from everyone around you | It is easy when everyone else around you is doing wrong | You should always copy what most of the people around you do | Doing right only matters when other people are watching you
exercise: What was the ark? | A huge wooden boat that carried Noah's family and the animals | A golden box kept in a temple | A tall tower made of bricks | A tent where Noah prayed in the desert
exercise: Why did the ark carry animals as well as people? | The story shows God cares about animals as well as people | Noah needed animals to row the boat | The animals were food for Noah's family for the whole long journey | Only animals could find the dry land
exercise: For how long did the rain fall in the story of the Flood? | Forty days | Seven days | Twelve months | Three days
exercise: What does the Flood story show about God? | Wrongdoing matters to him and he rescues those who trust him | He does not care what people do | He only cares about animals, not people | He punishes everyone exactly the same, whether they are good or bad
exercise: What did the dove bring back to Noah? | An olive leaf | A piece of bread | A small stone | A fish from the sea
exercise: What did the dove's olive leaf tell Noah? | The land was drying out | The rain was about to start again | The ark was about to sink | Another boat was nearby
exercise: What do the dove and olive branch stand for today? | Peace and new beginnings | War and danger coming soon | Rain, storms and bad weather | Gold, silver and great riches
exercise: What did the rainbow mean in the story? | God's promise never again to destroy the earth by flood | A warning that another flood was coming soon to cover the earth | The end of the world's colours | A sign that Noah should build a second ark
exercise: What does the rainbow promise teach about promises? | A promise is a serious thing that should be kept | Promises can be broken whenever it is easier to break them | Only small promises really need to be kept, not big ones | Promises count only if they are written down and signed
exercise: What is a covenant? | A solemn promise or agreement | A large boat made of wood | A song sung at a wedding | A type of tower made of bricks
exercise: Was the flood worldwide, according to Christians? | Some say worldwide, others a local flood or teaching story; it is disputed | All Christians agree it covered every mountain | All Christians agree it never happened at all | Christians say it was only a small puddle
exercise: What is the lesson of the flood story, whatever the history? | Trust in God and his faithfulness | That boats are better than houses | That rain is a punishment every time | That animals are more important than people
exercise: Why did people build the Tower of Babel? | To reach heaven and make a name for themselves | To keep all of the animals safe from the coming flood | To store grain for the seven years of famine in Egypt | To give homes to all the poor families of the city
exercise: What does the Tower of Babel teach about pride? | Wanting to be like God leads to division | Pride always makes people work well together | Building tall buildings is always wrong | Pride is a good thing if you are clever
exercise: What does the Babel story explain? | Why people speak different languages | Why the rainbow has seven colours in it | Why animals go in pairs | Why there is a day of rest
exercise: Which event do Christians see as the reversal of Babel? | Pentecost, when the good news was understood in every language | Christmas, when Jesus was born | The Flood, when the waters rose | The creation of the world in six days, when everything was made new
test: my sis says Noah took just one of every animal. right? | No, the story says pairs, so they could have young after | Yes, one of each was enough | No, he took only the birds | Yes, and all the rest of the animals swam along beside the ark
test: Everyone at the party is laughing at a new girl. You don't join in and they call you boring. Which Bible person does this remind you of? | Noah, who did right while everyone else did wrong | The builders of the Tower of Babel | Cain, who was jealous of his brother because God liked his gift | The serpent in the garden
test: Ahmed, who is Muslim, says: "We have Noah too, we call him Nuh." What is a good reply? | That's right, Noah is honoured in Islam as well as in the Bible | No, Noah is only in the Bible | Muslims made Noah up later | Nuh must be a completely different person
test: lol a rainbow is just light in raindrops, so the Bible's wrong. what can a Christian say | Science explains how a rainbow forms; for Christians it is also a sign of God's promise | Christians must say science is wrong about rainbows | Christians agree the Bible is wrong about everything | Rainbows only happened once, after the flood
test: Your friend sees an anti-war poster with a dove holding a twig and asks why. What do you tell her? | The dove with an olive branch has stood for peace since Noah's story | Doves are the fastest birds in war | Soldiers keep doves as pets in their camps | It is a poster about saving trees
test: My brother promised to help me and then went off with his mates, "it's just a stupid promise, chill". What does the rainbow story teach? | A promise is a serious thing, as God's promise is kept | Promises don't matter if you change your mind or something better comes up | Only God has to keep promises | A promise only counts if it is written down
test: A classmate says: "My uncle says Christians all believe the flood covered the whole world." What is more accurate? | Some do, others read it as a local flood or a teaching story | Yes, every Christian believes that | No Christian has ever believed it | Christians think it covered only Egypt
test: "We're the best class, we'll build the biggest snow fort ever and everyone will know OUR names!" They end up arguing and quit. Which story fits? | The Tower of Babel: pride and showing off led to division | Noah and the ark: obeying God | Creation: God rested on the seventh day after all his hard work | Adam and Eve hiding from God
test: Teacher asks: "Why do you think the dove came back the first time?" Which answer fits the story? | There was still no dry land to rest on | The dove did not like flying so far over water | Noah had called it back home for its dinner | The dove had lost the olive leaf on the way
test: Olympiad judge: "In the Bible, why is God's covenant important for how people trust him?" | Faith is a relationship built on promises God keeps | Covenants are just old contracts with no real meaning for people today | People only trust God when they are scared | A covenant means God never asks anything of people
test: Judge's follow-up: "How is Pentecost the opposite of Babel?" | At Babel languages divided people; at Pentecost everyone understood the good news in their own language | At Babel people spoke one language and at Pentecost no one spoke | Both stories end with a tower falling down | Pentecost is when the flood ended
exam: Identify the principal significance attributed to the rainbow in the narrative of the Flood. | A sign of the divine undertaking not again to destroy the earth by water | A portent of a further deluge yet to come | An indication that the ark was to be abandoned | A natural occurrence possessing no meaning whatsoever within the account
exam: Select the definition most accurately corresponding to the term "covenant" as employed in the Scriptures. | A solemn promise or agreement between parties | A vessel constructed for the conveyance of animals | A structure erected for the purpose of reaching heaven | A form of offering presented upon an altar
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Christian opinion as to the extent of the Flood? | It is disputed, some holding it universal and others local or figurative | It is uniformly held to have been universal | It is uniformly held to be entirely fictitious and without any foundation | It is uniformly held to have been confined to Egypt
exam: Ascertain the purpose for which, according to Genesis, the Tower of Babel was undertaken. | That its builders might reach heaven and make a name for themselves | That its builders might take refuge from the deluge should it return again | That grain might be stored therein against a famine of seven years' duration | That the languages of the nations might be preserved for all generations
